FINDING 1 — the dirty testdata fixture is BRANCH-INTRODUCED, not pre-existing.
  The brief pre-declares `M testdata/settings/whatif.json` after a test run as "a pre-existing suite
  defect". That premise is false, and I think the lead should know before relying on it.
    branch copy, file restored to the committed bytes first:
      $ go test ./... -count=1   -> exit 0 ("5897 passed in 50 packages")
        sha256(testdata/settings/whatif.json)[:16]  53bc146d6afe98a0 -> 2b02eb53e5095e26  CHANGED
    master tree (extracted read-only with `git archive master | tar -x`):
      $ go test ./... -count=1   -> exit 0 ("5667 passed in 50 packages")
        sha256[:16]  53bc146d6afe98a0 -> 53bc146d6afe98a0                                 UNCHANGED
  Bisected to the cmd/server tests that boot the app against testdata/ (not to the new whatif or
  analysis packages, which leave it clean):
      $ go test ./cmd/... -count=1      branch -> 2b02eb53e5095e26 ;  master -> unchanged
      per-test: TestWhatIf CHANGED, TestWhatIfProjectionChart CHANGED, TestDashboard CHANGED;
                TestSetupDependencies_Production and TestRun leave it clean.
  Mechanism, in internal/services/retirement/settings.go:397-402 (in the manifest):
      if _, present := rawFields["use_current_month"]; !present {
          settings.UseCurrentMonth = true
          changed = true
      }
      resolveCurrentMonth(settings, time.Now())
  A legacy settings file with no use_current_month key is migrated to true, marked changed, written
  back, and resolveCurrentMonth rewrites start_date to time.Now(). Correct and intended for a real
  user plan; on the tracked fixture it means (i) the suite is not idempotent and leaves the working
  tree dirty, and (ii) start_date in a COMMITTED fixture is now wall-clock dependent — today it was
  rewritten "2026-04" -> "2026-09", and a run next month writes that month instead. Observed diff
  also adds monthly_property_tax, birth_month, property_tax_inflation, rmd_timing and
  use_current_month: true. Per the brief I report rather than FAIL; flagging the mis-attribution so
  the lead can decide whether it needs a fix (e.g. pinning use_current_month:false in the fixture,
  as the SO1 fixture fix does in Go).

FINDING 2 — the evidence chart does not re-lay-out on a pure viewport resize.
  chartLayout sets autosize:false with an explicit width, and the ResizeObserver
  (whatif-spending-optimizer.js:644-648) only calls Plotly.Plots.resize, which cannot recompute an
  explicit width. Measured with a probe against the live-captured 468-month evidence:
      rendered at 1440   : {"layoutW":760,"svgW":760,"ticks":8,"docScrollW":1440,"innerW":1440}
      after PURE resize  : {"layoutW":760,"svgW":760,"containerW":347.5,"ticks":8,"docScrollW":375,"innerW":375}
      after theme toggle : {"layoutW":349,"svgW":349,"ticks":4,"docScrollW":375,"innerW":375}
      page overflows after pure resize: false
  So after rotating to a 375 px viewport the chart keeps a 760 px SVG inside a 347.5 px container
  with 8 year ticks (the 375 px bound is 4) until something else re-renders it. The page does NOT
  gain a horizontal scrollbar (scrollWidth stays 375) — the chart is cropped, not overflowing.
  Both repo harnesses happen to toggle the theme immediately after every resize, which forces the
  re-render, so neither of them can see this; I had to toggle the theme deliberately in my own probe
  to get past it. master's JS has no autosize:false chart, so the pattern is new here.
  Does not breach criterion 5 as written (tick density is correct at each width AS RENDERED).

FINDING 3 — coverage location, no action implied. Mutations (c), (d) and (e) leave
  ./internal/handlers/whatif entirely GREEN; only the analysis package catches them. The handler
  package's guard against the qualification rule and the seed-independence rule is therefore
  indirect. Relevant to checker-second's split-classification enumeration.

FINDING 4 — not verified by me, stated so explicitly: the verification document's runtime table
  (49.23 s / 111.39 s / 139.03 s, peak RSS 59-82 MiB) is a benchmark claim I did not reproduce; it
  is not among my five numbered criteria. The one timing I did observe end-to-end is the live
  harness's searchMs=38505 for a 2-position grid on copied real data. The document's own line
  "Copied-data browser checks remain pending" is now satisfied by 5a-5e above.

HARNESS GAPS I RELIED ON, disclosed: my tick probe runs the real controller in real Chromium with
real Plotly (not jsdom), so range-step and matchMedia caveats do not apply; but it renders the
optimizer form via page.setContent from the Go presentation fixture rather than the full /whatif
page, so page-level CSS cascade outside the fixture wrapper is not exercised there — 5c covers the
full page on the real server at 1440 and 375 in both themes and is where I take that evidence from.
